        "description": "## Overview\n\nAffected versions of this package are vulnerable to Insertion of Sensitive Information Into Sent Data via the `_sanitizeOptions` function in `index.js`, which uses `instanceof Array` to validate the `sensitiveHeaders` option. An attacker who controls the `sensitiveHeaders` value can supply a cross-realm array (created in a separate VM context) that passes `Array.isArray` but fails the `instanceof Array` check, causing the guard to reset `sensitiveHeaders` to an empty array and allowing sensitive headers to be forwarded to a redirect target that should not receive them.\n## PoC\nStart two loopback HTTP servers. The first returns a 302 redirect to the second. Send X-API-Key and list that name in sensitiveHeaders, then compare a normal array with a genuine cross-realm array created by:\n\n```js\nconst vm = require('node:vm');\nconst sensitiveHeaders = vm.runInNewContext(`['X-API-Key']`);\n```\n\nObserved against both source and the exact npm 1.16.1 package:\n\n- Same-realm ['X-API-Key']: the redirect target did not receive the key.\n- Cross-realm ['X-API-Key']: Array.isArray(value) was true, value instanceof Array was false, and the redirect target received X-API-Key.\n- An unrelated X-Harmless header remained present in both cases.\n\nThe complete 3.5 KB loopback-only harness and raw JSON output are available privately.\n\n**Note:** The PoC uses two loopback HTTP servers and a cross-realm array created via Node.js `node:vm`, tested against npm package version 1.16.1.\n## Remediation\nUpgrade `follow-redirects` to version 1.16.2 or higher.\n## References\n- [GitHub Advisory](https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p)\n- [GitHub Commit](https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/b0a7751e9cd64cd8eeb6ec67d2f206a9cc88d3f8)\n- [GitHub Release](https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/releases/tag/v1.16.2)\n",
